[Student Handbook › Section I – Preamble, Short Title, Commencement and Application › Definitions]
#### **Definitions**

In these Regulations, unless the context otherwise requires:

- a) “Academic Calendar” means the schedule of academic and miscellaneous events as approved by the Academic Council;

- b) “Academic Council” means the Academic Council of the University;

- c) “Academic Regulations” means the Academic Regulations of the University;

- d) “Academic Term” means a Semester or Summer Term;

- e) “Act” means the Vidyashilp University Act, 2020;

- f) “Board of Governors” means the Board of Governors of University as specified by the Act;

- g) “Board of Management” means the Board of Management of University as specified by the Act;

- h) “BoS” means the Board of Studies of a particular School / Department / Program of the University;

- i) “Clause” means duly numbered Clauses of these Regulations;

- j) “Course” means, a specific subject usually identified by its Course number and Course title, with specified credits and a Course Plan, taught by some teacher(s)/Course-instructor(s) to a specific class (group of students) during a specific academic term/semester;

- k) “Course Instructor” means, the teacher/faculty member or the Course instructor of a Course;

[Student Handbook › Section I – Preamble, Short Title, Commencement and Application › Definitions]
- l) “Dean” means the Dean of School / Centre of the University;

- m) “Degree Program” includes all Degree Programs;

- n) “Program Chair” means the Program Chair / Head of the Department / School within the University;

- o) “PAC” means, the Program Academic Committee of a concerned Program of Study of the University;

- p) “Program Regulations” means the Program Regulations governing a specific Degree Program offered by the University, and includes the set of Program-Specific Regulations, Program Structure and Curriculum;

- q) “Program Structure and Curriculum” means the components of the Program Curriculum, Courses with Credits and the mandatory minimum credits to be earned in each component for the award of the degree;

- r) “Registrar” means the Registrar of the University;

- s) “Registrar (Evaluation)” means the Registrar (Evaluation) of the University;

- t) “School/Department” means a constituent School / Department / Centre of the University established for monitoring, supervising and guiding, teaching, training and research activities in broadly related fields of studies;

- u) “Section” means duly numbered Sections of these Regulations;

[Student Handbook › Section I – Preamble, Short Title, Commencement and Application › Definitions]
- v) “Statutes” means the Statues of Vidyashilp University;

- w) “Sub-Clause” means duly numbered Sub-Clauses of these Regulations;

- x) "Summer Term” means an additional academic term conducted during the summer break (typically in June-July) for a duration of about eight (08) calendar weeks;

- y) “University / VU” means Vidyashilp University; and

- z) “Vice Chancellor” means the Vice Chancellor of the University.

[Student Handbook › Section IV – Code of Conduct › 3. Attributes of a Vidyashilp Graduate]
#### **3. Attributes of a Vidyashilp Graduate**

(What VU endeavors its students and alumni to BE!)

The unique “VU Experience” is a journey with an aspiration that each and every student of Vidyashilp will grow towards conscientious citizenship and exemplify the attributes of being:

- 3.1 **_Self-Aware:_** The student sojourn is an experience of self-discovery and selfknowledge. Explore one’s intellectual curiosity and humanity;

- 3.2 **_Confident:_** The self-awareness to manifest a developed sense of self-purpose and confidence to pursue one’s goals;

- 3.3 **_Empathetic:_** Being able to respect and appreciate other people; catalyze the free exchange of ideas in an interdependent, diverse community and explore ways to serve in harmony;

- 3.4 **_Resourceful:_** Take responsibility to apply knowledge, scholarship and creative practice to benefit society and the growth of the nation, and as a

- 3.5 **_Leader:_** Serve society with a commitment to the underprivileged and deprived in the community.

[Student Handbook › Section IV – Code of Conduct › 5. Failing to comply with this Code of Conduct will lead to and not limited to:]
#### **5. Failing to comply with this Code of Conduct will lead to and not limited to:**

- a) Written warning or reprimand;

- b) Payment of costs or compensation for any loss, damage or injury caused by the conduct;

- c) Issuance of an apology, made publicly or privately;

- d) Forfeiture of Scholarship, where applicable;

- e) Restriction or prohibition of access to, or use of, University facilities, services, activities or programs;

- f) Suspension and or expulsion from the University

